Art. 14.— The rule applies only to one octave and gives what are known as ¶ bearings,' whence the other notes must be derived by taking Octaves in the ¶ way.

Tuners so frequently get out in taking the ¶ that it is convenient to have a check ¶ the estimation of ear. This is furnished ¶ the fact that any note will beat the same ¶ of times in a second with an imperfect ¶ below and its Octave (that is, an im¶ Fifth) above. Thus if the note have ¶ vib., its imperfect Fourth below 300, and ¶ Octave above that Fourth below 600, the ¶ of the Fourth are 3x401-4x300 = 3, ¶ the beats of the Fifth are 3 x 401 - 2 x 600 ¶ 3 also. Now the imperfect Fourths are fur¶ by the bearings themselves. Thus, ¶ upwards, we have c'/' c", c'Jf/'jf c'% d' ¶ d", &c. Going downwards, the tuner takes ¶ Fifth and then a Fourth, as b' e' b, b'\f c'\y b\f, ¶ d' a, and so on from octave to octave. Mr. ¶ Smith prefers to insert the octaves ¶ when tuning the original bearings. Thus, ¶ the bearings were taken in the two-foot oc¶ c' c" as «' 10 d' 15 (/' 10 c' 15 /' 15 rt'J lOd'^ ¶ jr'J 10 c'% 15 ./"fl; 15 b' 10 c', he would intro¶ the octaves in this order, a a' d' d" ij' <j ¶ c" /' / aff a% d% d"ff g'^ (/}/; c'Jf c"Jf /'Jf /jj ¶ b' c' c" a'. But the method I have proposed ¶ simpler.

The principle of the check applies to the ¶ of other imperfect intervals, and

may serve as additional verifications. Thus a note beats equally with an imperfect minor Sixth below and its Octave the imperfect major Third above. Thus 500 : 801 beat 5 x 801

- 8 X 500 = 8, and 801 : 1000 beat 5 x 801 - 4 X 1000 = 8 also. Again, a note beats equally

with an imperfect minor Third below and its Octave the major Sixth above. Thus 500 : 601 beat 5x601-6x500 = 5, and 601 : 1000 beat 5 x 601 - 8 x 1000 = 5 also. If in each case we inverted the order, we should double the beats. Thus for the Fifth, 200 : 301 will beat 2 x 301

- 3 X 200 = 2 ; but 301 : 400 will beat 4 x 301 - 3

X 400 = 4. For the major Third, 400 : 501 will ffy beat 4x501-5x400 = 4; but 501:800 will beat 8 X 501 - 5 X 800 = 8. For the major Sixth, .300 : 501 will beat 3 x 501 - 5 x .300 = 3 ; but ¶ 501 : 600 will beat 6 x 501 - 5 x 600 = 6. reason is obvious. The fractions expressing the minor intervals 4, f , i have odd denominators and even numerators, and hence their inversions reduce by dividing by 2, but this is ¶ not the case for the major intervals, f, f ,

So much of the beauty of tuning pianos, harmoniums, and organs depends on the perfection of the Octave, that tuners would do well to apply the first test with the Fourth below and Fifth above, as a matter of course.

Art. 15. — Rule for tuning in meantone temperaiuent from c 252-7, HatidrVs ¶ to c 283-6, Father Smith's pitch for the Durham organ.

As will be seen by the table p. 434&, c'264 ¶ Helmholtz's pitch is a small meantone ¶ and the Durham pitch is a mean¶ Tone, sharper than Handel's. The great ¶ of the Fifths in the meantone intona¶ makes it necessary to divide the tunings ¶ three classes, sufficiently ascertainable by ¶ fork in Helmholtz's or even in French pitch. ¶ first is from rather less than a Semitone ¶ about a Quartertone flatter than French ¶ ; the second is French pitch and from a ¶ flatter to a Quartertone sharper;

¶ the third is from a Quartertone to a Semitone sharper than French.

The rule would extend to 27 notes, but on the proof it will be carried out only for 14 notes, as on the Elnglish concertina, for which ¶ this intonation is still used. And for this strument the ' tuning octave ' may be taken as (■' to c". For the few organs that still use this intonation the same Octave must be tuned, and hence, when taken on the ' principal,' the digitals must be fingered from c to <•', because the beats would be otherwise too rapid to count.

