===
Art. 14.—The rule applies only to one octave and gives what are known as 'the bearings,' whence the other notes must be derived by taking Octaves in the usual way.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
Tuners so frequently get out in taking the Octaves that it is convenient to have a check on the estimation of ear. This is furnished by the fact that any note will beat the same number of times in a second with an imperfect Fourth below and its Octave (that is, an @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
imperfect Fifth) above. Thus if the note have 401 vib., its imperfect Fourth below 300, and the Octave above that Fourth below 600, the beats of the Fourth are 3 × 401 − 4 × 300 = 3, and the beats of the Fifth are 3 × 401 − 2 × 600 = 3 also. Now the imperfect Fourths @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
are furnished by the bearings themselves. Thus, going upwards, we have *c' f' c''*, *c'*♯ *f'*♯ *c''*♯, *d' g' d''*, &c. Going downwards, the tuner takes a Fifth and then a Fourth, as *b' e' b*, *b'*♭ *e'*♭ *b*♭, *a' d' a*, and so on from octave to octave. Mr. Hermann @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
Smith prefers to insert the octaves above when tuning the original bearings. Thus, if the bearings were taken in the two-foot octave *c' c''* as *a'* 10 *d'* 15 *g'* 10 *c'* 15 *f'* 15 *a'*♯ 10 *d'*♯ 15 *g'*♯ 10 *c'*♯ 15 *f'*♯ 15 *b'* 10 *e'*, he would introduce the @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
octaves in this order, *a a' d' d'' g' g c' c'' f' f a*♯ *a'*♯ *d'*♯ *d''*♯ *g'*♯ *g*♯ *c'*♯ *c''*♯ *f'*♯ *f*♯ *b b' e' e'' a'*. But the method I have proposed seems simpler.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
The principle of the check applies to the inversions of other imperfect intervals, and may serve as additional verifications. Thus a note beats equally with an imperfect minor Sixth below and its Octave the imperfect major Third above. Thus 500 : 801 beat 5 × 801 − 8 × @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
500 = 8, and 801 : 1000 beat 5 × 801 − 4 × 1000 = 8 also. Again, a note beats equally with an imperfect minor Third below and its Octave the major Sixth above. Thus 500 : 601 beat 5 × 601 − 6 × 500 = 5, and 601 : 1000 beat 5 × 601 − 3 × 1000 = 5 also. If in each case we @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
inverted the order, we should double the beats. Thus for the Fifth, 200 : 301 will beat 2 × 301 − 3 × 200 = 2; but 301 : 400 will beat 4 × 301 − 3 ¶ × 400 = 4. For the major Third, 400 : 501 will beat 4 × 501 − 5 × 400 = 4; but 501 : 800 will beat 8 × 501 − 5 × 800 = 8. @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
For the major Sixth, 300 : 501 will beat 3 × 501 − 5 × 300 = 3; but 501 : 600 will beat 6 × 501 − 5 × 600 = 6. The reason is obvious. The fractions expressing the minor intervals 4/3, 8/5, 6/5 have odd denominators and even numerators, and hence their inversions reduce @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
by dividing by 2, but this is not the case for the major intervals, 3/2, 5/4, 5/3.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
So much of the beauty of tuning pianos, harmoniums, and organs depends on the perfection of the Octave, that tuners would do well to apply the first test with the Fourth below and Fifth above, as a matter of course.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
Art. 15.—*Rule for tuning in meantone temperament from c'* 252·7, *Handel's pitch, to c'* 283·6, *Father Smith's pitch for the Durham organ.*

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
As will be seen by the table p. 434b, *c'*264 or Helmholtz's pitch is a small meantone Semitone, and the Durham pitch is a meantone Tone, sharper than Handel's. The great flatness of the Fifths in the meantone intonation makes it necessary to divide the tunings into @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
three classes, sufficiently ascertainable by a fork in Helmholtz's or even in French pitch. The first is from rather less than a Semitone to about a Quartertone flatter than French pitch; the second is French pitch and from a Quartertone flatter to a Quartertone @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
sharper; ¶ the third is from a Quartertone to a Semitone sharper than French.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
The rule would extend to 27 notes, but on the proof it will be carried out only for 14 notes, as on the English concertina, for which this intonation is still used. And for this instrument the 'tuning octave' may be taken as *c'* to *c''*. For the few organs that still @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
use this intonation the same Octave must be tuned, and hence, when taken on the 'principal,' the digitals must be fingered from *c* to *c'*, because the beats would be otherwise too rapid to count.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
Tune in the following order the numbers 25-6-7 and 40-1-2, meaning that the beats are to be 25 and 40 for the low, 26 and 41 for the medium, and 27 and 42 for the high pitch in 10 sec., according to the three grades already laid down.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
	*c'* 25-6-7 *g'* 40-1-2 *d'* 25-6-7 *a'* 40-1-2 *e'* 25-6-7 *b'* 40-1-2 *f'*♯ 40-1-2 *c'*♯ 25-6-7 ¶ *g'*♯ 40-1-2.
	*c'* 40-1-2 *f'* 40-1-2 *b'*♭ 25-6-7 *e'*♭ 40-1-2 *a'*♭.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
The tuning is conducted in the same way as before, only in two series, from *c'* to *g'*♯ and from *c'* to *a'*♭, making the Fifths and Fourths at first perfect, and then the Fifths closer and the Fourths wider. But in two cases *c'f'*, *f'b'*♭ the Fourth is taken @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
upwards, and then the upper note has to be sharpened. And there is an additional verification after tuning to *e'*, for the major Thirds *c'e'*, *g'b'*, *d'f'*♯, *a'c'*♯, *e'g'*♯, and also *f'a'* and minor Sixths *d'b'*♭, *c'a'*♭ should be all sensibly perfect. Octaves @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
can be verified by imperfect Fourths more easily than in equal temperament.

@@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
The equations for determining the pitch from the beats are \[\begin{array}{rcl} 2g' & = & 3c' - 2{\cdot}5\text{-}{\cdot}6\text{-}{\cdot}7 \\ 4d' & = & 3g' - 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \\ 2a' & = & 3d' - 2{\cdot}5\text{-}{\cdot}6\text{-}{\cdot}7 \\ 4e' & = @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
& 3a' - 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \\ 2b' & = & 3e' - 2{\cdot}5\text{-}{\cdot}6\text{-}{\cdot}7 \\ 4f'\sharp & = & 3b - 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \\ 4c'\sharp & = & 3f'\sharp - 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \\ 2g'\sharp & = & @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
3c'\sharp - 2{\cdot}5\text{-}{\cdot}6\text{-}{\cdot}7 \\ 4d'\sharp & = & 3g'\sharp - 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \\ \hline 3f' & = & 4c' + 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \\ 3b'\flat & = & 4f' + 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \\ @@R@@
>>>
<<<
@@R@@
===
3e'\flat & = & 2b'\flat + 2{\cdot}5\text{-}{\cdot}6\text{-}{\cdot}7 \\ 3a'\flat & = & 4e'\flat + 4{\cdot}0\text{-}{\cdot}1\text{-}{\cdot}2 \end{array}\]
